<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murong De</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">336–405</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> De was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> prince, younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a> of Later Yan, and founder of Southern Yan. When Northern Wei took the Later Yan capital Zhongshan in 397, he led followers south, proclaimed himself King of Yan in 398 and later emperor, ruling from Guanggu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murong-de.jpg" alt="Murong De" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: see below · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harvard_drs_49802792_%E7%B9%A1%E5%83%8F%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E6%BC%94%E7%BE%A9%E7%BA%8C%E7%B7%A8_v.1_(%E6%85%95%E5%AE%B9%E5%BE%B7).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
